{
  "text": "Let E be any real normed vector space, let V : E → ℝ be any real-valued function, and let α be any real number, without a sign restriction. Suppose V is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable on all of E and, for every point x ∈ E and every vector v ∈ E, its second derivative satisfies\n\nα ‖v‖² ≤ D²V(x)[v,v].\n\nThen V is α-strongly convex on the entire space E: the domain E is convex, and for every x,y ∈ E and all real numbers a,b ≥ 0 with a+b = 1,\n\nV(a x + b y) ≤ a V(x) + b V(y) − (α/2) a b ‖x−y‖².\n\nThe norm is the given norm of E, and all scalar multiplications are real. Here D²V(x)[v,v] means first differentiating the continuous-linear-map-valued function x ↦ DV(x), then applying its derivative to v, and applying the resulting real-valued continuous linear map to v again. The underlying Fréchet-derivative operation is totalized to zero where differentiability fails, but the assumed C² regularity ensures that the first and second derivatives appearing here are genuine derivatives. Both the derivative bound and the conclusion are pointwise and global. No completeness, finite-dimensionality, inner-product structure, measurable structure, or measure is assumed.",
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "Let E be any real normed vector space, let V : E → ℝ be any real-valued function, and let α be any real number, without a sign restriction. Suppose V is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable on all of E and, for every point x ∈ E and every vector v ∈ E, its second derivative satisfies\n\nα ‖v‖² ≤ D²V(x)[v,v].\n\nThen V is α-strongly convex on the entire space E: the domain E is convex, and for every x,y ∈ E and all real numbers a,b ≥ 0 with a+b = 1,\n\nV(a x + b y) ≤ a V(x) + b V(y) − (α/2) a b ‖x−y‖².\n\nThe norm is the given norm of E, and all scalar multiplications are real. Here D²V(x)[v,v] means first differentiating the continuous-linear-map-valued function x ↦ DV(x), then applying its derivative to v, and applying the resulting real-valued continuous linear map to v again. The underlying Fréchet-derivative operation is totalized to zero where differentiability fails, but the assumed C² regularity ensures that the first and second derivatives appearing here are genuine derivatives. Both the derivative bound and the conclusion are pointwise and global. No completeness, finite-dimensionality, inner-product structure, measurable structure, or measure is assumed.",
